WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.065
- The Internet can be a dark place,

00:00:03.065 --> 00:00:06.482
but occasionally there's a spot of light.

00:00:08.787 --> 00:00:11.342
One day, I was sitting
here at work at BuzzFeed,

00:00:11.342 --> 00:00:13.498
and I get this strange email.

00:00:13.498 --> 00:00:15.052
It's someone just telling
me about their life,

00:00:15.052 --> 00:00:16.641
telling me about how they
have book club tomorrow,

00:00:16.641 --> 00:00:19.282
how the tree outside of
their window is blooming

00:00:19.282 --> 00:00:21.264
so spring must be here,
and telling me about

00:00:21.264 --> 00:00:24.439
how their 102nd birthday is this weekend.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:26.066
102nd birthday.

00:00:26.066 --> 00:00:27.580
So I showed it to my desk neighbor, Ben,

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.001
and I was like, dude Ben,
what's the deal with this email

00:00:30.001 --> 00:00:31.750
from this lady?

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.033
And Ben looked over, and was
like, wow, that's adorable.

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:38.405
At the end of the email, it's signed Bea.

00:00:38.405 --> 00:00:39.952
B-E-A.

00:00:39.952 --> 00:00:41.936
I don't believe in anything spiritual,

00:00:41.936 --> 00:00:43.643
but my grandmother's name was Bea,

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:45.192
and of all the members of my family,

00:00:45.192 --> 00:00:47.756
I would say I am the most
similar to my grandma.

00:00:47.756 --> 00:00:49.821
She was a writer, I'm a writer.

00:00:49.821 --> 00:00:50.882
When my grandma turned 80,

00:00:50.882 --> 00:00:53.542
I even portrayed her in a family play.

00:00:53.542 --> 00:00:55.993
Grandma Bea was a very
extremely important figure

00:00:55.993 --> 00:00:58.242
in my life, and here I
am getting this email

00:00:58.242 --> 00:00:59.912
from a woman named Bea.

00:00:59.912 --> 00:01:02.596
So there was something too
magical about this to ignore.

00:01:02.596 --> 00:01:04.302
I said, hello Bea, I believe you have

00:01:04.302 --> 00:01:06.985
the wrong email address, though
your book club and kitchen

00:01:06.985 --> 00:01:08.895
both sound quite lovely.

00:01:08.895 --> 00:01:11.042
Happy birthday, cheers, Jordan.

00:01:11.042 --> 00:01:12.503
The magic of this was not lost on me,

00:01:12.503 --> 00:01:13.767
but I figured I'd leave it at that.

00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:16.120
So the next day, I get another email.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:17.861
Again, she tried to
email this other Jordan,

00:01:17.861 --> 00:01:19.283
and again she got me.

00:01:19.283 --> 00:01:20.664
So again, I let her know.

00:01:20.664 --> 00:01:22.571
As I get more emails,
I'd show them to Ben.

00:01:22.571 --> 00:01:24.545
He'd be like, that's adorable, too!

00:01:24.545 --> 00:01:28.682
So then the third time, Bea
tried to get the other Jordan

00:01:28.682 --> 00:01:31.604
and wrote, I must have sent
with a wrong address once.

00:01:31.604 --> 00:01:34.006
Now when I start both addresses come up.

00:01:34.006 --> 00:01:35.143
You are my only one.

00:01:35.143 --> 00:01:37.013
Still sunny, Bea.

00:01:37.013 --> 00:01:39.701
That email she also meant
for the other Jordan.

00:01:39.701 --> 00:01:41.288
He was her only one, not me.

00:01:41.288 --> 00:01:42.668
So I had to tell her yet again, hi Bea,

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:44.871
I'm afraid you still
have the wrong Jordan.

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:46.851
She responded, to whoever Jordan,

00:01:46.851 --> 00:01:48.692
I'm not sure I'll ever get you sorted out.

00:01:48.692 --> 00:01:50.834
My 101 years has caught up with me.

00:01:50.834 --> 00:01:52.337
And I felt so bad.

00:01:52.337 --> 00:01:54.452
Bea was starting to get frustrated,

00:01:54.452 --> 00:01:55.671
and I was worried she would give up

00:01:55.671 --> 00:01:57.538
and not contact the right Jordan.

00:01:57.538 --> 00:01:58.797
So I started looking for him myself.

00:01:58.797 --> 00:02:00.917
My last name, spelled like it's spelled

00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:02.463
is extremely uncommon.

00:02:02.463 --> 00:02:03.681
Brian Imbrey, my dad.

00:02:03.681 --> 00:02:04.657
Jordan Imbrey, me.

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:05.988
Aviva Imbrey, my sister.

00:02:05.988 --> 00:02:06.821
Amber Imbrey, my sister-in-law.

00:02:06.821 --> 00:02:07.654
Tyler Imbrey, my brother.

00:02:07.654 --> 00:02:09.096
Mary Imbrey, my mom.

00:02:09.096 --> 00:02:10.311
Shane Imbrey, who lives in Utah.

00:02:10.311 --> 00:02:12.670
(speaking very quickly)

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.142
And as far as I can see,
those are the only Imbreys

00:02:15.142 --> 00:02:16.440
that exist on Facebook.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:20.788
That's only eight Imbreys
in the world on Facebook,

00:02:20.788 --> 00:02:22.780
and somehow she's accidentally
getting this Jordan Imbrey.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.335
So I start looking for
it without the middle E

00:02:24.335 --> 00:02:26.088
or with an E at the beginning,

00:02:26.088 --> 00:02:29.917
every spelling you can think
of, and I can't find anything.

00:02:29.917 --> 00:02:30.975
Then I have an idea.

00:02:30.975 --> 00:02:33.373
Mine's Imbrey, I-M-B-R-E-Y.

00:02:33.373 --> 00:02:37.540
And I tried searching I-M-B-E-R-Y.

00:02:38.478 --> 00:02:40.448
Jordan Imbery.

00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:41.896
And this guy exists.

00:02:41.896 --> 00:02:43.729
I thought I was totally
alone in this world

00:02:43.729 --> 00:02:45.475
in terms of having my exact name,

00:02:45.475 --> 00:02:48.185
and this guy almost has my exact name.

00:02:48.185 --> 00:02:50.510
- My name is Jordan Imbery.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.115
I'm 22, and I was born and raised

00:02:53.115 --> 00:02:54.915
in Langley, British Columbia.

00:02:54.915 --> 00:02:57.498
I currently live in Chilliwack.

00:02:59.301 --> 00:03:02.137
I found out about Bea
through Ancestry DNA.

00:03:02.137 --> 00:03:04.251
We connected as distant relatives.

00:03:04.251 --> 00:03:06.363
So it told us that we
had a shared ancestor

00:03:06.363 --> 00:03:07.301
in the recent past.

00:03:07.301 --> 00:03:08.801
We got in contact.

00:03:19.166 --> 00:03:21.605
- We've been in touch
since last year, December.

00:03:21.605 --> 00:03:24.087
She'll write me and tell
me about how her day went.

00:03:24.087 --> 00:03:25.466
She'll tell me about her book clubs,

00:03:25.466 --> 00:03:27.343
moles that appear in her garden.

00:03:27.343 --> 00:03:29.093
I gave her advice how to get of them.

00:03:29.093 --> 00:03:32.431
I've never met anybody with
a similar name like Imbery

00:03:32.431 --> 00:03:34.039
or the same name.

00:03:34.039 --> 00:03:36.611
- So I contacted Jordan
Imbery on Facebook and said,

00:03:36.611 --> 00:03:39.700
Hey, I think a 102 year
old woman named Bea

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.291
is trying to contact you.

00:03:41.291 --> 00:03:43.283
And it turns out it's right,
but he's also never met her.

00:03:43.283 --> 00:03:45.397
Frankly, I wasn't willing
to just say that's the end

00:03:45.397 --> 00:03:46.370
of this story.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.213
So I asked Bea if she'd
like to be pen pals.

00:03:49.213 --> 00:03:51.534
Asking someone to be your
pen pal is like asking them

00:03:51.534 --> 00:03:53.768
to commit time and effort to you,

00:03:53.768 --> 00:03:55.433
and why should she care about me?

00:03:55.433 --> 00:03:58.317
I'm just some guy who
she emailed by accident

00:03:58.317 --> 00:03:59.614
who she has no relation to,

00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:03.360
but luckily Bea is a very
kind and social person,

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.396
and she agreed to be my pen
pal, and we've corresponded

00:04:05.396 --> 00:04:07.646
for months ever since then.

00:04:19.366 --> 00:04:21.121
- So Bea and I have been
corresponding since April,

00:04:21.121 --> 00:04:22.666
occasionally writing each other.

00:04:22.666 --> 00:04:24.793
She tells me all about
her big extended family

00:04:24.793 --> 00:04:27.259
and whichever grandkid's
birthday is coming up

00:04:27.259 --> 00:04:29.988
ad whatever big parties they have planned.

00:04:29.988 --> 00:04:32.877
I tell her about my weird life
making videos at BuzzFeed.

00:04:32.877 --> 00:04:35.482
I had an opportunity
through work to go to Mumbai

00:04:35.482 --> 00:04:38.247
for awhile and Bea was
actually instrumental

00:04:38.247 --> 00:04:40.771
in helping me decide to
take that opportunity.

00:04:40.771 --> 00:04:43.206
- I think he'd enjoy Mumbai
more because it's exotic

00:04:43.206 --> 00:04:44.585
and farther from home, and maybe he

00:04:44.585 --> 00:04:46.752
had not been there before.

00:04:48.809 --> 00:04:50.348
- And so then I got to thinking,
I've been corresponding

00:04:50.348 --> 00:04:53.153
with Bea since April, Jordan
Imbery has corresponding

00:04:53.153 --> 00:04:55.022
with her for a while longer,

00:04:55.022 --> 00:04:56.816
neither of us have met her in person.

00:04:56.816 --> 00:04:58.483
Neither of us have met each other.

00:04:58.483 --> 00:04:59.316
Why don't we do it?

00:04:59.316 --> 00:05:00.757
We're gonna go to Bea's house.

00:05:00.757 --> 00:05:02.827
We're gonna go to her book club.

00:05:02.827 --> 00:05:04.373
We're gonna have a big
dinner with her family,

00:05:04.373 --> 00:05:05.916
and we're all just gonna
get to know each other.

00:05:05.916 --> 00:05:07.427
I don't really know what to expect.

00:05:07.427 --> 00:05:09.055
- What I wanna get out of this experience

00:05:09.055 --> 00:05:11.297
meeting Bea would probably
be her life experience

00:05:11.297 --> 00:05:14.635
of what is was like
growing up in the 20s, 30s,

00:05:14.635 --> 00:05:16.837
40s, 50s, her secret to her longevity

00:05:16.837 --> 00:05:19.170
because I wanna live to 105.

00:05:22.375 --> 00:05:24.613
- I'm super excited and nervous for me,

00:05:24.613 --> 00:05:25.967
the guy with the same name as me,

00:05:25.967 --> 00:05:29.634
and my 102 year old pen
pal to all meet IRL.

00:05:31.517 --> 00:05:33.057
- Jordan Imbery.

00:05:33.057 --> 00:05:35.161
- Hey, Jordan Imbrey.

00:05:35.161 --> 00:05:36.870
- Yeah, nice to meet you in real life.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:37.703
- You too.

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:41.671
- Is it pronounced Im-berry
or Im-burry or Im-berrie?

00:05:41.671 --> 00:05:43.056
- Im-burry or Im-berry.

00:05:43.056 --> 00:05:44.152
- But it's not Im-berrie?

00:05:44.152 --> 00:05:44.985
- No.

00:05:44.985 --> 00:05:45.818
- No one says Im-berrie?

00:05:45.818 --> 00:05:46.651
- Sometimes.

00:05:46.651 --> 00:05:47.484
- So we're different.

00:05:47.484 --> 00:05:49.396
- I get called Im-berrie
sometimes, it's not correct.

00:05:49.396 --> 00:05:50.229
- You correct people.

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:51.467
You're like Im-berrie is a bullshit name.

00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:52.636
My name is Imbery.

00:05:52.636 --> 00:05:54.019
- Mine's a better name.

00:05:54.019 --> 00:05:55.524
- How long has this name
been in your family?

00:05:55.524 --> 00:05:56.459
- Hundreds of years.

00:05:56.459 --> 00:05:57.292
- Really?

00:05:57.292 --> 00:05:59.311
Okay yours has been around longer.

00:05:59.311 --> 00:06:00.530
Mine is an Ellis Island change.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.115
A lot of Jews that came from
Eastern Europe and stuff

00:06:02.115 --> 00:06:04.838
would change their name so
they'd sound more American.

00:06:04.838 --> 00:06:07.081
- Pennsylvanian Dutch Mennonite roots.

00:06:07.081 --> 00:06:09.281
They moved to Waterloo,
Ontario and from there

00:06:09.281 --> 00:06:11.235
my family went to B.C.

00:06:11.235 --> 00:06:13.472
Her family went to Washington state.

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:15.305
- Any big thing you're hoping
to learn from meeting her?

00:06:15.305 --> 00:06:16.951
- I would like to learn
what she's been eating

00:06:16.951 --> 00:06:19.535
that's given her that longevity

00:06:19.535 --> 00:06:21.979
cause I would like to
make it to the year 2100.

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:22.925
- Okay.

00:06:22.925 --> 00:06:25.374
You think the biggest
thing she has to teach us

00:06:25.374 --> 00:06:27.298
is how to eat healthy?

00:06:27.298 --> 00:06:28.852
We got books on that already.

00:06:28.852 --> 00:06:31.501
I want some profound
insight about what it is

00:06:31.501 --> 00:06:35.775
to be a human but I think
that's a lot to ask of someone.

00:06:35.775 --> 00:06:37.527
- I wanna know what it was
like to I guess grow up

00:06:37.527 --> 00:06:38.867
in the dirty thirties, too.

00:06:38.867 --> 00:06:40.330
- What do you think it was like?

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:41.163
- Depressing maybe?

00:06:41.163 --> 00:06:42.646
- She was alive during both world wars.

00:06:42.646 --> 00:06:43.663
That's nuts.

00:06:43.663 --> 00:06:45.057
- When she was emailing
you she messaged me

00:06:45.057 --> 00:06:46.725
and she's like I wrote your email wrong.

00:06:46.725 --> 00:06:48.026
I've been writing this
Jordan in California.

00:06:48.026 --> 00:06:51.882
So I guess you don't know
that my flowers are blooming.

00:06:51.882 --> 00:06:53.564
I guess I don't, right?

00:06:53.564 --> 00:06:55.068
- Did it annoy you a
little bit that some other

00:06:55.068 --> 00:06:57.181
Jordan Imbrey was coming
in and starting to pen pal

00:06:57.181 --> 00:06:58.681
with your pen pal?

00:06:59.786 --> 00:07:01.700
- The more the merrier.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:02.533
- You're a good guy.

00:07:02.533 --> 00:07:05.450
- We're getting into the woods now.

00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:07.476
This house?

00:07:12.493 --> 00:07:13.709
- What's the tree that supposed to always

00:07:13.709 --> 00:07:15.133
be blooming over here?

00:07:15.133 --> 00:07:17.531
Oh yeah M cause their
last name starts with M.

00:07:17.531 --> 00:07:18.912
- One of the things that's
crazy about being 102

00:07:18.912 --> 00:07:21.332
is that her kids are in their 70s.

00:07:21.332 --> 00:07:25.890
- It's Jordan and honorary
Jordan, honorary cousin.

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:28.902
She's been looking forward
to it since I think August

00:07:28.902 --> 00:07:31.187
when you came back and
started talking about it.

00:07:31.187 --> 00:07:33.836
It has been a big deal for the family too

00:07:33.836 --> 00:07:35.683
cause it's a big deal to her.

00:07:35.683 --> 00:07:36.820
This is what she lives for.

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:37.956
Big moments.

00:07:37.956 --> 00:07:39.666
New adventures.

00:07:39.666 --> 00:07:42.483
- So we're hanging tight in Bea's garage

00:07:42.483 --> 00:07:45.971
while our camera crew sets
up for us to meet her.

00:07:45.971 --> 00:07:47.447
This is Bea's car.

00:07:47.447 --> 00:07:51.660
Kirk said she hasn't driven
it since she was 99 years old.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.459
She's now 102.

00:07:53.459 --> 00:07:55.092
That's a long time to drive.

00:07:55.092 --> 00:07:56.438
Let's look at that interior.

00:07:56.438 --> 00:07:57.579
Nice red burgundy.

00:07:57.579 --> 00:07:59.291
- Chrome trim.

00:07:59.291 --> 00:08:00.124
- [Jordan] Chrome trim.

00:08:00.124 --> 00:08:01.240
- Just wondering if you think Bea

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:02.675
is a hugger or handshaker?

00:08:02.675 --> 00:08:04.346
- We don't actually know her.

00:08:04.346 --> 00:08:06.012
We only know her by email.

00:08:06.012 --> 00:08:07.884
So what's appropriate?

00:08:07.884 --> 00:08:09.570
- Greetings.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:10.504
- Is that what you're gonna say?

00:08:10.504 --> 00:08:11.357
You're gonna say greetings?

00:08:11.357 --> 00:08:12.614
- No.

00:08:12.614 --> 00:08:14.978
- Mom, you got a couple of Jordans here.

00:08:14.978 --> 00:08:16.074
- Oh.

00:08:16.074 --> 00:08:16.907
- Why hello.

00:08:16.907 --> 00:08:17.740
- Both Jordans?

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:18.761
- Yeah, this is California.

00:08:18.761 --> 00:08:20.019
- Oh okay.

00:08:20.019 --> 00:08:21.102
- And Canada.

00:08:22.231 --> 00:08:24.062
- Well I could tell you that
you're not the same size.

00:08:24.062 --> 00:08:26.191
So I can tell a difference.

00:08:26.191 --> 00:08:28.222
- So one's official and one's an honorary.

00:08:28.222 --> 00:08:30.639
- Nice to meet you in person.

00:08:31.595 --> 00:08:33.420
- I'm glad to meet all you people.

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:34.253
- Nice to meet you.

00:08:34.253 --> 00:08:35.731
- Oh gosh yeah.

00:08:35.731 --> 00:08:36.795
- A hug?

00:08:36.795 --> 00:08:38.215
- Yes.

00:08:38.215 --> 00:08:39.132
Absolutely.

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:46.736
You can't imagine how much
excitement it's created

00:08:46.736 --> 00:08:50.644
and I have this friend that
is a deacon of genealogy.

00:08:50.644 --> 00:08:53.035
She sent this one little
email and I answered

00:08:53.035 --> 00:08:55.312
and there you were.

00:08:55.312 --> 00:08:57.075
That's what created all this.

00:08:57.075 --> 00:08:58.212
- How long have you been doing book club?

00:08:58.212 --> 00:09:00.854
- I think I've only been
in about eight years.

00:09:00.854 --> 00:09:02.031
- That's a lot of books.

00:09:02.031 --> 00:09:03.660
- I'm not gonna finish the book this time.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:05.656
I'm not reading as fast as I thought I was

00:09:05.656 --> 00:09:09.399
and I'm just not gonna do the end of it.

00:09:09.399 --> 00:09:10.456
- Got rid of your moles?

00:09:10.456 --> 00:09:13.298
- Yeah there's one track left out there.

00:09:13.298 --> 00:09:17.556
The little black box out in
the far side of the lawn.

00:09:17.556 --> 00:09:18.405
- 1915.

00:09:18.405 --> 00:09:19.873
- What's your secret?

00:09:19.873 --> 00:09:21.132
- I think mine's the good genes, you know?

00:09:21.132 --> 00:09:22.271
That's just the way it is.

00:09:22.271 --> 00:09:23.819
Just before my 100th birthday

00:09:23.819 --> 00:09:25.075
I was going through immigration.

00:09:25.075 --> 00:09:27.715
Man looked at my passport and looked at me

00:09:27.715 --> 00:09:29.829
and he looked at my passport
and gave it back to me.

00:09:29.829 --> 00:09:32.831
He left his chair and came
around and stooped down

00:09:32.831 --> 00:09:36.983
eye level with me and he says
were you really born in 1915?

00:09:36.983 --> 00:09:38.971
I says I was and everybody
within hearing distance

00:09:38.971 --> 00:09:40.600
stopped and looked.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.062
- What was your favorite decade?

00:09:42.062 --> 00:09:42.995
- I don't know.

00:09:42.995 --> 00:09:45.639
When I was younger I
used to hope I would live

00:09:45.639 --> 00:09:49.420
to see the 2000 on the
calendar and here I am.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:51.331
Gone through a lot of
calendars since then.

00:09:51.331 --> 00:09:53.610
- Could you tell us a
little bit about your house?

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:57.193
- I moved into it in
June the 10th in 1941.

00:09:59.587 --> 00:10:01.054
My dad built the house.

00:10:01.054 --> 00:10:03.864
- This is about Bea Matthews
and this kinda sums it up.

00:10:03.864 --> 00:10:04.919
One day this guy came by.

00:10:04.919 --> 00:10:08.049
It was in the evening and he
wanted coal, a sack of coal.

00:10:08.049 --> 00:10:09.884
- It was a dollar a sack.

00:10:09.884 --> 00:10:11.756
And he says I can go down at the corner

00:10:11.756 --> 00:10:13.908
and get it for 90 cents.

00:10:13.908 --> 00:10:15.656
I says that's the place to get it.

00:10:15.656 --> 00:10:16.508
Well they're closed.

00:10:16.508 --> 00:10:18.055
But you'd talk me out of 10 cents

00:10:18.055 --> 00:10:19.440
if you could, wouldn't you?

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.685
- Bea had also made a comment
about where's your husband?

00:10:21.685 --> 00:10:24.167
I wouldn't have my wife
out here doing this

00:10:24.167 --> 00:10:26.813
and she says no but you'd
chisel me outta 10 cents.

00:10:26.813 --> 00:10:28.196
- Having lived 102 years would you say

00:10:28.196 --> 00:10:30.080
you lived carefully?

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:32.600
Did you smoke, drink, stay out late?

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:33.986
- No I didn't do those things.

00:10:33.986 --> 00:10:37.923
When I was growing up it was
a meat and potatoes type.

00:10:37.923 --> 00:10:39.266
That's the way people lived.

00:10:39.266 --> 00:10:42.158
- So when you emailed me
by accident the first time

00:10:42.158 --> 00:10:44.322
and then I later asked if
you would be my pen pal

00:10:44.322 --> 00:10:46.158
did you think that was really weird?

00:10:46.158 --> 00:10:48.399
Were you like why does this
guy wanna be my pen pal?

00:10:48.399 --> 00:10:49.872
- Well why not?

00:10:49.872 --> 00:10:51.796
- When you were our age
what were you doing?

00:10:51.796 --> 00:10:52.731
So I'm 25.

00:10:52.731 --> 00:10:53.564
He's 21.

00:10:54.439 --> 00:10:55.618
I'm 26.

00:10:55.618 --> 00:10:58.201
(all laughing)

00:10:59.422 --> 00:11:00.399
- He's 22 now.

00:11:00.399 --> 00:11:02.714
- I got both of our ages wrong.

00:11:02.714 --> 00:11:04.581
So what were you doing at 22?

00:11:04.581 --> 00:11:06.825
- Well I had just had my
first child and she's 80

00:11:06.825 --> 00:11:08.952
years old now, or almost.

00:11:08.952 --> 00:11:09.866
- Do you remember what you were doing

00:11:09.866 --> 00:11:11.575
when you were 26 years old?

00:11:11.575 --> 00:11:12.408
- I don't know.

00:11:12.408 --> 00:11:13.241
That's too much.

00:11:13.241 --> 00:11:14.217
You figure it out.

00:11:14.217 --> 00:11:15.875
- What would you say in your lifetime

00:11:15.875 --> 00:11:19.731
is the biggest culture
shift that you can remember?

00:11:19.731 --> 00:11:20.867
- Well I think it's almost right now

00:11:20.867 --> 00:11:22.691
the lack of respect that people give

00:11:22.691 --> 00:11:26.873
that the younger generation
gives to our older people

00:11:26.873 --> 00:11:27.971
and the kids that go by here

00:11:27.971 --> 00:11:30.181
very seldom do I ever get eye contact.

00:11:30.181 --> 00:11:31.653
They're punching these things

00:11:31.653 --> 00:11:33.073
and they're stuffing their ears.

00:11:33.073 --> 00:11:34.874
- Took her to the emergency room.

00:11:34.874 --> 00:11:36.989
We were there like til two in the morning.

00:11:36.989 --> 00:11:39.327
Broken one and two?

00:11:39.327 --> 00:11:40.660
- One and three.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.240
- One and three which
normally would probably be it.

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:48.499
So what she'd done since she
fell at seven in the morning,

00:11:48.499 --> 00:11:50.285
she went down and did her laundry twice

00:11:50.285 --> 00:11:52.520
cause she had blood on her stuff

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:54.707
and she wanted to be cleaner for that.

00:11:54.707 --> 00:11:56.458
- [Bea] Then I just
learned about two days ago

00:11:56.458 --> 00:11:58.727
that we have a unit
available for me to move

00:11:58.727 --> 00:12:00.407
into assisted living.

00:12:00.407 --> 00:12:03.025
- [Jordan] Bea has lived in the same house

00:12:03.025 --> 00:12:04.525
for over 70 years.

00:12:05.795 --> 00:12:07.715
- In 1972 we bought this finished,

00:12:07.715 --> 00:12:10.715
had a little bit of remodeling done.

00:12:11.799 --> 00:12:13.556
It's a Washington territory.

00:12:13.556 --> 00:12:15.257
The carpeting is all maple.

00:12:15.257 --> 00:12:19.016
My kids'll remember skating
on it with the blacks

00:12:19.016 --> 00:12:20.479
when we'd polish it.

00:12:20.479 --> 00:12:23.481
- What again is your relationship
to Bea and to Thatcher?

00:12:23.481 --> 00:12:25.760
- Third cousins, four times removed.

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.328
Fifth cousins, twice removed.

00:12:28.328 --> 00:12:29.384
- [Man] So this is Bea.

00:12:29.384 --> 00:12:30.217
- [Man] That's Bea.

00:12:30.217 --> 00:12:31.431
- [Man] And you're here.

00:12:31.431 --> 00:12:32.823
- [Bea] The baby in that
picture is Thatcher.

00:12:32.823 --> 00:12:35.156
- That little baby Thatcher?

00:12:37.259 --> 00:12:39.384
- That's the dogwood right behind her.

00:12:39.384 --> 00:12:40.323
- [Man] That's the dogwood.

00:12:40.323 --> 00:12:42.077
- That's the legendary dogwood

00:12:42.077 --> 00:12:44.499
that started this whole thing.

00:12:44.499 --> 00:12:45.690
- [Bea] Can't believe it.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:47.481
Who writes about their dogwood?

00:12:47.481 --> 00:12:48.314
- Ready?

00:12:52.624 --> 00:12:53.957
Come on fishies.

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:56.233
Not interested.

00:12:56.233 --> 00:12:58.452
- Bea's gonna take us to her book club.

00:12:58.452 --> 00:13:02.072
Jordy, has Bea talked to you
a lot about her book club?

00:13:02.072 --> 00:13:02.905
- No.

00:13:02.905 --> 00:13:03.985
- Do you read?

00:13:03.985 --> 00:13:04.883
- Not at all.

00:13:04.883 --> 00:13:06.957
- Me either pretty much.

00:13:06.957 --> 00:13:09.000
Maybe they'll inspire us
to get back into reading.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.279
- [Man] Regular or decaf?

00:13:11.279 --> 00:13:12.112
- Decaf.

00:13:12.112 --> 00:13:13.356
I didn't know the
difference between decaf.

00:13:13.356 --> 00:13:15.364
- So how often do you all have book club?

00:13:15.364 --> 00:13:16.259
- [All] Once a month.

00:13:16.259 --> 00:13:17.393
- We were both saying
we're hoping you guys

00:13:17.393 --> 00:13:19.317
will inspire us to read more.

00:13:19.317 --> 00:13:20.498
Who chooses?

00:13:20.498 --> 00:13:22.049
- [Woman] We all do.

00:13:22.049 --> 00:13:22.984
- [Woman] We just wing it.

00:13:22.984 --> 00:13:25.545
- Well we have somebody that
always reads one or two.

00:13:25.545 --> 00:13:30.543
- Is there a certain genre
you guys like to stick to?

00:13:30.543 --> 00:13:32.857
- It's the last hurrah because
next month I'll be moving.

00:13:32.857 --> 00:13:35.136
I spent two hours
yesterday at Miro Gardens

00:13:35.136 --> 00:13:36.109
answering all the questions.

00:13:36.109 --> 00:13:37.774
- My grandmother's name was Bea.

00:13:37.774 --> 00:13:40.383
She was a big writer and
reader and a participant

00:13:40.383 --> 00:13:42.050
in book clubs for a lot of her life.

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:45.360
- Did you find the author's
narrative style effective?

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:48.046
I hadn't thought much about the style.

00:13:48.046 --> 00:13:49.346
- [Woman] It was very readable.

00:13:49.346 --> 00:13:51.781
- Well Dickens knew that
he had a good thing going

00:13:51.781 --> 00:13:54.266
but I'm sure he never
realized the great lengths

00:13:54.266 --> 00:13:56.191
it was gonna go to.

00:13:56.191 --> 00:13:59.858
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:14:01.926 --> 00:14:04.343
- [Woman] What you got there?

00:14:07.931 --> 00:14:11.550
- Jordan never got the email
that you meant to send him

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.751
originally which came to me
so we thought it'd be fun

00:14:13.751 --> 00:14:16.155
to have you read him the original email.

00:14:16.155 --> 00:14:18.856
- Hi Jordan, from a new Dell computer.

00:14:18.856 --> 00:14:20.113
Newer are supposed to be easier.

00:14:20.113 --> 00:14:22.115
The touch is different
but I'll keep at it.

00:14:22.115 --> 00:14:24.066
The pink dogwood I see
from the kitchen window

00:14:24.066 --> 00:14:26.628
and the lilac by the
front door are in bloom.

00:14:26.628 --> 00:14:27.850
I guess spring is close.

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:30.287
I'm preparing for book
club here next Tuesday.

00:14:30.287 --> 00:14:31.402
There are 11.

00:14:31.402 --> 00:14:34.743
We meet at nine a.m. and
serve a light breakfast.

00:14:34.743 --> 00:14:36.902
The book is Vanity Fair by Thackery.

00:14:36.902 --> 00:14:39.955
The book is 640 pages small print.

00:14:39.955 --> 00:14:41.871
I looked it up in the synopsis.

00:14:41.871 --> 00:14:44.010
Otherwise another birthday
coming next week as well.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:45.677
I can't believe 102!

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:48.481
Oh the sun I coming out.

00:14:48.481 --> 00:14:49.538
Time to get to work.

00:14:49.538 --> 00:14:50.871
Have a good day.

00:14:51.734 --> 00:14:53.028
Bea.

00:14:53.028 --> 00:14:54.251
- That was your first email?

00:14:54.251 --> 00:14:55.801
What were you thinking?

00:14:55.801 --> 00:14:57.304
- Then you were confused, huh?

00:14:57.304 --> 00:14:58.137
- Yeah.

00:14:58.137 --> 00:14:59.417
How does it make you feel to
know that this frustration

00:14:59.417 --> 00:15:01.453
was going on just trying to talk to you?

00:15:01.453 --> 00:15:02.536
- I feel bad.

00:15:04.501 --> 00:15:07.556
I was okay with it as long as Bea replies

00:15:07.556 --> 00:15:09.951
to my emails first.

00:15:09.951 --> 00:15:12.113
- You're okay with me
jumping in as long as

00:15:12.113 --> 00:15:13.210
you get priority?

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:14.893
You're family, I'm not.

00:15:14.893 --> 00:15:16.925
I'm just some guy on the internet.

00:15:16.925 --> 00:15:18.104
- New family.

00:15:18.104 --> 00:15:21.521
Were you aware of the names were similar?

00:15:22.493 --> 00:15:24.301
- No, we'd never heard of each other.

00:15:24.301 --> 00:15:26.415
- I searched my name,
he would always come up

00:15:26.415 --> 00:15:27.475
cause he's more well known right?

00:15:27.475 --> 00:15:30.937
So he's like did you mean Jordan Imbrey?

00:15:30.937 --> 00:15:31.770
- I swore in this one.

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:32.748
Damn.

00:15:32.748 --> 00:15:34.455
I carefully checked before I sent.

00:15:34.455 --> 00:15:36.046
Will try to find my original letter.

00:15:36.046 --> 00:15:36.879
Bea.

00:15:38.408 --> 00:15:42.075
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:15:49.953 --> 00:15:53.120
- [Jordan] Your favorite place to eat?

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:56.239
- [Woman] How do you
pronounce your last name?

00:15:56.239 --> 00:15:57.132
- Imbrey.

00:15:57.132 --> 00:15:57.965
- Im-berry.

00:15:57.965 --> 00:15:59.737
- I propose a toast.

00:15:59.737 --> 00:16:02.154
A toast to grandma, G.G. Bea.

00:16:04.608 --> 00:16:06.191
- [Bea] Thank you.

00:16:06.191 --> 00:16:07.330
- [Woman] And the Jordans!

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:08.497
- And Jordans.

00:16:09.649 --> 00:16:10.946
We're very happy you being here

00:16:10.946 --> 00:16:13.599
and being pen pals with grandma

00:16:13.599 --> 00:16:15.188
wonderful relationship and we're all happy

00:16:15.188 --> 00:16:17.338
that you guys are all here.

00:16:17.338 --> 00:16:18.722
Everyone with.

00:16:18.722 --> 00:16:19.658
- [All] Cheers!

00:16:19.658 --> 00:16:23.227
- Could you break down for
us sort of how the connection

00:16:23.227 --> 00:16:24.488
happened one more time?

00:16:24.488 --> 00:16:25.872
How you connected these two?

00:16:25.872 --> 00:16:26.801
- Certainly.

00:16:26.801 --> 00:16:31.322
We've made up her family
tree and put it on Ancestry.

00:16:31.322 --> 00:16:33.275
- [Jordan] How did that come about?

00:16:33.275 --> 00:16:34.944
- She and I worked together and her family

00:16:34.944 --> 00:16:36.369
has done a lot of research.

00:16:36.369 --> 00:16:40.236
So I just had to incorporate
it in to Ancestry.

00:16:40.236 --> 00:16:43.766
Once her results came back,
then it talks about matches

00:16:43.766 --> 00:16:47.933
and anybody that has a
potential match will show up

00:16:49.549 --> 00:16:53.782
and Jordan emailed me so I
said that I would contact Bea

00:16:53.782 --> 00:16:56.631
and ask her if it was
okay for them to talk

00:16:56.631 --> 00:17:01.507
and Bea's great at doing
emails and so she writes back

00:17:01.507 --> 00:17:03.865
and that communication started.

00:17:03.865 --> 00:17:05.782
- I just wanna say thank you to all of you

00:17:05.782 --> 00:17:07.976
for being such generous hosts to us

00:17:07.976 --> 00:17:09.196
and welcoming both of us as family.

00:17:09.196 --> 00:17:10.292
He really is family.

00:17:10.292 --> 00:17:14.125
And I'm just some guy
and you're welcoming me.

00:17:17.273 --> 00:17:19.628
- Well I'm lucky that
you were both so nice.

00:17:19.628 --> 00:17:20.929
- Jordan's a couple years younger than me

00:17:20.929 --> 00:17:23.162
and I never had a younger sibling.

00:17:23.162 --> 00:17:25.765
So it kinda felt like
having a little brother.

00:17:25.765 --> 00:17:27.836
- I think I'll stay in
touch with Jordan Imbrey.

00:17:27.836 --> 00:17:30.514
We've thrown out that idea.

00:17:30.514 --> 00:17:31.931
Maybe we should switch lives.

00:17:31.931 --> 00:17:34.753
You make windows and I make film.

00:17:34.753 --> 00:17:36.217
I expected nothing less from the best

00:17:36.217 --> 00:17:39.798
when meeting Bea's family and
they treated us like family.

00:17:39.798 --> 00:17:41.832
Bea's a very kind hearted person.

00:17:41.832 --> 00:17:43.335
Fun to be around.

00:17:43.335 --> 00:17:46.845
You'd never notice the 80 year age gap.

00:17:46.845 --> 00:17:50.928
I feel like I talk to her
like I talk to anybody.

00:18:04.248 --> 00:18:07.698
- At the end of all of this
I feel really fulfilled.

00:18:07.698 --> 00:18:09.119
Made two new great friends.

00:18:09.119 --> 00:18:11.119
- We spend a lot of time
right now focusing on distrust

00:18:11.119 --> 00:18:12.829
for our fellow humans.

00:18:12.829 --> 00:18:16.036
Try hard to remember how
many people are out there

00:18:16.036 --> 00:18:17.781
who just wanna connect
with other good people,

00:18:17.781 --> 00:18:19.812
feel love and give love,

00:18:19.812 --> 00:18:23.434
and I was really reminded
of that this week.

00:18:23.434 --> 00:18:25.755
All the internet is is
a tool that connects us.

00:18:25.755 --> 00:18:26.850
It can connect us in bad ways

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:29.252
but it can also connect
us in beautiful ways.

00:18:29.252 --> 00:18:30.507
- I feel on top of the world.

00:18:30.507 --> 00:18:32.136
It's hard to tell you the lift I felt

00:18:32.136 --> 00:18:35.506
just knowing that you have
met and don't travel a lot

00:18:35.506 --> 00:18:37.537
and to have my world come to me.

00:18:37.537 --> 00:18:39.406
It's pretty uplifting
and I know something more

00:18:39.406 --> 00:18:42.073
is gonna happen, something good.

00:18:43.368 --> 00:18:45.267
- Oh absolutely.

00:18:45.267 --> 00:18:47.350
I have maybe five of you.

00:18:50.093 --> 00:18:52.366
- I came in here feeling
a little bit like worried

00:18:52.366 --> 00:18:56.094
that I had invaded this
private thing you two had had

00:18:56.094 --> 00:18:59.260
and I could not have felt
more wrong about that

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:02.143
once I saw how warm and
welcoming you were to me

00:19:02.143 --> 00:19:04.050
and a friendship that we developed too.

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:05.956
- Bea, you were everything
I expected to be.

00:19:05.956 --> 00:19:07.663
You're an amazing woman.

00:19:07.663 --> 00:19:09.686
Jordan, you're an awesome person too

00:19:09.686 --> 00:19:12.297
and I hope we keep in
touch and you're my new

00:19:12.297 --> 00:19:13.720
pen pal as well.

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.072
- Well I think it's great
that you two have finally met.

00:19:16.072 --> 00:19:19.079
Sounds like you each acquired a new friend

00:19:19.079 --> 00:19:22.285
as well as I have
acquired several new ones.

00:19:22.285 --> 00:19:24.682
Both of you have lived
up to my expectations

00:19:24.682 --> 00:19:26.477
and maybe beyond a little.

00:19:26.477 --> 00:19:27.897
- This is so nice.

00:19:27.897 --> 00:19:29.147
- I need a hug.

00:19:30.409 --> 00:19:32.441
And one for you.

00:19:32.441 --> 00:19:34.226
- [Jordan] So glad to be
able to come meet you, Bea.

00:19:34.226 --> 00:19:35.080
- Oh it's fun.

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:35.913
I'm glad you two met.

00:19:35.913 --> 00:19:37.287
It's been a great day.

00:19:37.287 --> 00:19:38.997
- I like you, Jordan.

00:19:38.997 --> 00:19:41.580
(all laughing)

00:19:42.944 --> 00:19:44.444
That was awkward.

